{
  "id": "rotting-log",
  "name": "Life on a rotting log",
  "group": "From food webs",
  "citation": {
    "text": "Savely, H. E. (1939). Ecological relations of certain animals in dead pine and oak logs. Ecological Monographs, 9(3), 321–385; Mangal networks 1494 to 1497.",
    "url": "https://doi.org/10.2307/1943233"
  },
  "datasetIds": ["mangal:1494", "mangal:1495", "mangal:1496", "mangal:1497"],
  "description": "Animals that Savely found inside dead oak and pine logs in Duke Forest, North Carolina, in the 1930s, cut open a year after the trees died and again after three years or more: springtails and ants that persist through every stage, bark beetles and their predators in the fresh pine, and the millipedes, snails and termites of the softened wood.",
